  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 PM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nOvernight heavy rainfall in northern Georgia has led to significant \nriver flooding on the Ocoee River. The good news is that rain has \ncome to an end, and portions of the Ocoee have already crested. The \nflood wave is still moving through remaining portions of the river as\nit heads toward the Hiwassee. Continue to heed all statements \nfrom the City of Copperhill and the remaining flood warnings \nassociated with the flood wave.\n\nThe rest of today and tomorrow are both mild and dry, with patchy \ndense fog fairly likely tomorrow morning as we continue to dry out \nfrom the rainfall. An upper trough in the Great Lakes will push a \nweak cold front through the region tomorrow afternoon and evening. \nThis will yield a slightly cooler airmass going forward, with \ntemperatures near normals for November.\n\nThe front tomorrow should be a dry or mainly dry front, though I can \nsee some light rain showers or drizzle as the HRRR depicts a \nsaturated, but shallow profile underneath a deeper dry airmass \nfarther aloft. Added some low rain chances along the northern \nPlateau for this. No significant weather expected with this front. \nPost front, temperatures will cool to near or just below seasonable \naverages, and remain mild into next week, with some patchy frost \npossible for many locations each morning. Dry weather is likely to \ncontinue into next week, outside of the already mentioned \ndrizzle/light rain along the cold front tomorrow.",
